    <statement><p>Suppose that we use Euler's method to approximate the
solution to the differential equation
<me>\frac{dy}{dx}= \frac{x^3}{y};\qquad y( 0.1) = 2.</me>
Let <m>f(x,y) = x^3/y.</m></p>
<p>We let <m>x_0 = 0.1</m> and <m>y_0 = 2</m> and pick a step size <m>h=0.2 .</m></p>
<p>Euler's method is the the following algorithm. From <m>x_n</m> and <m>y_n ,</m>
our approximations to the solution of the differential
equation at the nth stage,
we find the next stage by computing
<me>x_{n+1} = x_n +h,\qquad y_{n+1} = y_n + h\cdot f(x_n,y_n).</me></p>
<p>Complete the following table.  Your answers should be accurate to at least seven decimal places.</p>
